\fs30 \cf2 \
A desktop mood-tracking app built with Python and Tkinter. Users can log moods, write a private diary, and view mood statistics.\
\
## Features\
- Create an account, log in and log out\
- Track your mood from 15 options, with an optional note\
- Personal diary with saved entries\
- Statistics graph with time-period filters\
- Admin dashboard protected by a one-time code\
\
## Requirements\
- Python 3.8 or newer (Tkinter included on Mac and Windows)\
- Packages in `requirements.txt` (pillow, matplotlib, pyotp)\
\
## Setup\
    pip install -r requirements.txt\
    python3 digital_wellbeing.py\
\
Keep the `PICS/` folder in the same folder as `digital_wellbeing.py`.\
\
## Database\
The app uses SQLite, which is built into Python, so there is nothing to install. On first run it creates `digital_wellbeing.db` on your own device. Each installation has its own separate, local database, and no data is shared online. To view or edit your data, open the file with the `sqlite3` command-line tool or DB Browser for SQLite and use standard SQL.\
\
## Admin login (optional)\
On first run, a popup shows a setup key. Add it to an authenticator app (Google Authenticator, Authy) using "Enter a setup key" (time-based). Then log in to Admin Mode with the username `admin` and the current 6-digit code.\
\
## Notes\
- User passwords are stored as plain text. This is a learning project and should not be used for sensitive data.\
- `digital_wellbeing.db` and `admin_otp_secret.txt` are created locally and are not part of this repository.\
